# ec/tools/grade_0751_isolation.py --self-test, wired into the cheap gate.
# Prepared here rather than landed, for the reason
# docs/ci/agent-gates-capture-claims.patch gives: `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh`
# is copied from the agent-pipeline template and the pipeline's push token has no
# `workflow` scope, so a branch editing it fails at the end of the PR rather than
# at the start of it. `docs/agent-pipeline.md` carries this one across a re-copy
# too, as its item 7.
#
# To land it:
#
#   git apply docs/ci/agent-gates-0751-self-test.patch
#
# and that is the whole change.
#
# Re-cut whole against the committed `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh` (blob
# 125d647) on 2026-09-25, issue #745. The previous cut did not apply at all,
# and BOTH its hunks were broken, not the one the issue named. The tool-list
# hunk needed three contiguous lines that stopped being contiguous when item
# 8's citation_gap_scan.py and xdata_register_map.py landed in the same list.
# The case-arm hunk needed the `*)` arm as its trailing context, which sat
# three lines below it and now sits at :238, with the whole citation_gap_scan
# and xdata_register_map arms in between -- `git` stops reporting at the first
# failing hunk, which is what made it look like the tool list alone. It now
# carries an `index` line, which it did not have: a future `--3way` has nothing
# to work from without one.
#
# Why the cheap tier and not agent-gates-deep.sh: what the mode reads is the
# committed CSVs under `ec/tools/testdata/` and the standard library's
# `unittest`. No firmware image, no Ghidra, no network, no assembler -- so by the
# split `docs/agent-pipeline.md` records, it belongs beside
# `check_register_counts`, not in the deep tier. It measures 0.25 s end to end
# here (0.25-0.26 s over five runs; the 103 tests are 0.160 s of that, the rest
# interpreter start), against a cheap tier recorded at 5.9 s, on 2026-09-25.
# That is one runner's figure and the ratio is the point; `docs/agent-pipeline.md`
# item 6 gives the same caveat for `call_graph.py`.
#
# What it buys, and what it does not: the grader's 103 committed tests are the
# pinned record of a dozen refusal policies, and `grep -rn 'grade_0751'
# .github/` finds nothing, so a PR that breaks one merges green. **Until a human
# lands this, nothing runs the suite per commit, and a PR that breaks one of its
# refusals still merges green.** No line of that patch, of the mode, or of the
# write-up beside it is a claim about a laptop: the suite reads hand-built
# fixtures, and no EC is opened and no register read back.
#
# The 103 is what `python3 ec/tools/grade_0751_isolation.py --self-test` prints
# on the tree this was cut from. It was 76 when this patch was first written, and
# 94 by the time issue #745 was filed; neither was ever right for long, which is
# the point of reading it off a run. The other occurrences of the figure are
# issue #685's to correct -- deliberately not here, so this issue and that one
# cannot both be editing the same sentences. They are
# `docs/findings/0751-grader-self-test-gate.md`, which carries a count in six
# places as a per-commit chain each figure is correct for, and
# `docs/findings.md:2077` ("Those 76 tests ran nowhere", in §7), which is
# historical -- past tense, about issue #532's own branch at the time -- and not
# a live claim. The plan behind this cut also expected two tool docstrings
# carrying the figure; a search of `ec/tools/*.py` and `windows/tools/*.py` found
# none, because `grade_0751_isolation.py` parses the count out of the run rather
# than asserting one.
#
# The comment it adds at the call site says the same thing where the next reader
# of the gate will see it, rather than only here.

diff --git a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
index 125d647..9d80607 100755
--- a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
+++ b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
@@ -123,6 +123,7 @@ check_ghidra_tooling() {
               ec/tools/grade_name_basis.py \
               ec/tools/group_functions.py \
               ec/tools/call_graph.py \
+              ec/tools/grade_0751_isolation.py \
               ec/tools/citation_gap_scan.py \
               ec/tools/xdata_register_map.py \
               bios/tools/bios_extract.py \
@@ -189,6 +190,27 @@ check_ghidra_tooling() {
       *call_graph.py)
         python3 "$tool" --check && python3 "$tool" --self-test || rc=1
         ;;
+      # The 0x0751 isolation grader's refusals, and the one tool here that runs
+      # its own unittest suite rather than a hand-written list of known
+      # answers: grade_0751_isolation.py --self-test discovers
+      # test_grade_0751_isolation.py in that tool's own directory and runs it in
+      # a subprocess, which is what keeps two copies of one file out of one
+      # interpreter. The tool takes a required capture positional and has
+      # neither --check nor a --work dir, so --self-test alone is the whole of
+      # it here -- the *) default below would hand it flags it does not have.
+      # What it holds is a dozen refusal policies, each of which is a gate
+      # between a human's hardware day and a wrong §7 call: a mark set that
+      # does not hold, a void block, a withheld window, a --block that named no
+      # block, a capture given twice, a mark no block can be attributed to. A
+      # PR that breaks one of them merges green today. Cheap tier because of
+      # what it reads: committed CSVs under ec/tools/testdata/ and the standard
+      # library's unittest -- no firmware image, no Ghidra, no network, no
+      # assembler. It prints its own deferral, that none of it is hardware
+      # evidence, and it asserts no test count: the exit code decides, for the
+      # reason tools/run-tests.sh gives.
+      *grade_0751_isolation.py)
+        python3 "$tool" --self-test || rc=1
+        ;;
       # The call graph's own blind spot, measured. It reads the bytes Ghidra's
       # function boundary cut out of a citing listing and returns one of three
       # verdicts per (callee, citer) pair, so the 99-row population
